#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa3dc44f-86e9-58c8-96da-3f517ff52007" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Triple Integral as a Mixed or Repeated Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070f7b7d-4de6-59d3-8631-f2f1dd96090f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344231} {:answer/id #uuid "ac513e85-3ea0-5377-8564-d9e202173bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344232} {:answer/id #uuid "93887add-252d-5b7d-ae86-15a575f894eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+xy+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344233} {:answer/id #uuid "7456e408-cfa2-5ab0-8417-8be0c717e02b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344234} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea23d3f-5ab8-533a-976a-1c4db5d09553" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7456e408-cfa2-5ab0-8417-8be0c717e02b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344234} :answer-field/id #uuid "780a5649-9207-53ae-9fbc-ca4e87b29e38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26712122-d955-52e5-a48d-0c18c4785850" :question/math-academy-id "q-161495" :question/problem "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nis defined over the type I region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2},\\,-2\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 1+xy}.\n$$\n\nUse $[a,b]$ as the full nonempty projection of the exactly specified region $R$ onto the $y$ axis. The inner limits may be piecewise. What is $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "The inequalities defining $R$ are retained exactly. For every $y\\in[-2,4]$, choosing $x=0$ leaves the nonempty fiber $0\\le z\\le1$. Thus the full nonempty $y$ projection is $[a,b]=[-2,4]$ and $a+b=2$.\n\nThe base must also satisfy $1+xy\\ge0$. When $y<0$, this requires $x\\le-1/y$. The bounds $y^2$ and $-1/y$ meet at $y=-1$. Hence\n\n$$\\iiint_R f\\,dV=\\int_{-2}^{-1}\\int_0^{-1/y}\\int_0^{1+xy}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy+\\int_{-1}^4\\int_0^{y^2}\\int_0^{1+xy}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy.$$\n\nOn the first branch the $x$ cap is $-1/y$; on the second it is $y^2$. The source solution used $x\\le y^2$ throughout and therefore included reversed $z$ bounds for some negative $y$. Its isolated expression $1+xz$ is also a transcription error: the upper $z$ bound is $1+xy$. These solution errors do not change the correct endpoint sum $2$."}]}
